<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis I, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1173">1173</a>–<a href="/years/1231">1231</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Kelheimer · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis I, called the Kelheimer, was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> of the house of Wittelsbach from 1183 to 1231. In 1214 Emperor Frederick II granted him the Palatinate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, which his family secured by marrying his son Otto II to the Welf heiress Agnes, beginning nearly six centuries of Wittelsbach rule there. He founded towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> and was murdered on the bridge at Kelheim in 1231.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis, called the Kelheimer because he was born and died at Kelheim, was the only surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a>, the first Wittelsbach duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>. After his father died in 1183 his mother took over the regency and secured his inheritance, and he came of age in 1189. In the following years he fought a war against Count Albert III of Bogen and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-i-of-bohemia">Ottokar I of Bohemia</a> over the Sulzbach lands, until Emperor Henry VI intervened in 1193. In return Louis stayed on the side of the Staufen for the next decade and a half, and in 1192 he received knighthood at Worms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Henry's death a double election in 1198 split the empire, and Louis supported the Hohenstaufen Philip of Swabia. In 1204 he married Ludmilla of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the widow of Albert of Bogen, gaining an alliance with her uncle King Ottokar I. That year the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> died without heirs and King Philip gave Louis major areas, with which he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> and began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a>. After Philip's murder in 1208 Louis seized the March of Istria, then made terms with King Otto IV, but in 1211 rejoined the Hohenstaufen party. In 1214 King Frederick II rewarded him with the Palatinate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and his son Otto II married Agnes of the Palatinate.</p><p>Louis took the crusader vow at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 1215 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1218. In May 1221 he sailed to Damietta with a Bavarian army. After the defeat at Mansurah in August 1221 he was given as a hostage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a> but was released under a truce negotiated by Frederick's envoys. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isar">Isar</a> in 1224.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1225 Louis became guardian of the young king Henry, but relations with both his ward and the emperor deteriorated, and in a conflict of 1229 he was defeated in the field. He withdrew to Kelheim Castle in 1230. In 1231 he was murdered on a bridge in Kelheim. The killer, reportedly an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassin</a>, was lynched at once, so the crime was never cleared up, though many suspected Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. Kelheim lost the Wittelsbach family's favour and its status as a ducal residence. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious">Otto the Illustrious</a> had the bridge pulled down the next year and turned its gate into a chapel. Louis was buried in the crypt of Scheyern Abbey. The lion became a heraldic symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Palatinate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis I, Duke of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kelheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Kelheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.92,11.87&amp;year=1210" aria-label="Kelheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis I, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.92,11.87&amp;year=1210">What was here in 1210? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
